    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Continuum mechanics models can be used to map the stress and strain fields developing in a sedimentary basin during extension driven by the major faults in the underlaying basement or within the overburden. In particular it is possible to explain the growth of secondary thrust faults and their trajectories as a result of the compressional fields developing in the sedimentary cover. These features are often observed in sand box model experiments and are sometimes reported also in nature within the hanging wall of normal faults. It is also possible to use the continuum mechanics models to predict the extent and the local of the deformation caused in the sedimentary sequence by slip of the faults in the basement. The amount of dip of the faults in the basement seems to be of paramount importance in controlling the geometry and shape of the faults developing in the sedimentary overburden.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
